an see what  we're doing is we're trying to look for  the date - Bob's tag and if we have that  we split it either by dashes or slashes  and then we look for a time universal  time tag if that's not there we look for  a time obstacle nastiness but luckily we  can put this all together into a date  object and then convert the date object  directly to a Julian date using  functions which are built into  Mathematica so we got the Julian data  anyway so one more bit of complexity  care so now we want to be able to  extract the brightness as a function of  time so we have this this function here  photometry jobs and this will give it a  Fitz object will give it a list of  coordinates in rows and columns where  the star is located a comparision star  coordinates because we've got to make  these observations relative to a non  varying star and the magnitude of the  non Varian star and then the diameter of  the star we're looking at the gap  diameter which is the outer part of the  gap and the sky annulus diameter  so this is pretty straightforward though  first we get the fits object the image  object out of the image we get the  Julian date out of the fits object and  then we do our photometry calculation on  the star and this will give us the  magnitude of the star based on the star  diameter the gap in the sky diameter and  we'll do the same thing to the reference  star find the difference in magnitudes  and then we'll return a list comprised  of the Julian date and the differential  magnitude plus the comparison star  magnitude so now we can now finally we  got those tools put together we can we  can actually crunch some data so right  now I'm using data that came from the  University of Iowa some of our example  data for studying Sophia's  so now we can just use Mathematica to  put all this stuff together so we get  our file names of our directory of our  fits objects here there's star dot FTS  I've loaded the Cepheid images and this  is the region of the sky we're looking  at and University of Iowa has kindly  pointed out where the variable star is  and where a reference star is and we can  take a look at the first of our images  so there's the starfield and if you look  carefully so there's our Cepheid and  there's a reference start over there and  we can pick up the coordinates of this  simply by just clicking on it and using  our coordinates tool to pick out the  rows and columns of our target star and  our reference star so once we got that  we can just and assuming the star fields  are all well aligned we can compute the  photometry on each one of the images and  this will take a few seconds here but  it's pulled those in and it's done the  computation with the reference star at  magnitude 10 the the diameter of the  star mask six pixels an 8 pixel diameter  gap a 20 pixel diameter annulus and it  sorted those by the observation time so  they're all in sequence although not  necessarily evenly spaced and that's a  significant thing let's see yeah so the  first of the data points here you can  see the Julian date is two point four  five four five four times ten to the six  so about two point four five million  days since that time in 4000 BC the  magnitude of the star is ten point seven  two five two at that observation time so  now we're getting somewhere we can  actually plot the light curve and the  way I like to do it is I take the I take  the first date and I subtract those off  and if we plot that we  get the light curve of the star over a  period of twenty or thirty days and you  can see that the period of this and this  is a classic Cepheid light curve the  period is about fourteen some days this  is list lying plot so list line plot I  can I can do just a list plot and let's  see what that looks like oh yeah yeah  yeah right yeah so that that's our data  without the lines it's a little harder  to see the pattern there but that's like  the line plot so if we so now we want to  find out what the what the period is  b